// ProjectDeliveryS349 — Sprint 349: 70 items waited on the human with the console down, because serve
// holds the binary I kept rebuilding. Serve a copy; make the turn boundary notice. EXECUTE. 3 pts.
package ProjectDeliveryS349 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    #CharteredBy dependency from oversightAdvisoryStory to d0093;

    part oversightAdvisoryStory : Story {
        :>> id = "0d800580-0814-6580-9b3e-000000300580";
        :>> title = "Sprint 349: the turn boundary notices when the human's console is down and work is waiting (issue150, 3 pts)";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> kind = WorkKind::code; :>> priority = WorkPriority::p0; :>> owner = "claudeOpus5"; :>> estimatedPoints = 3;
    }

    action def DeliveryRunS349 {
        action storyOversightAdvisory;
        verification storyOversightAdvisoryDoD : Test { :>> id = "0d800581-0814-6581-9b3e-000000300581"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "THE HUMAN HAD TO ASK WHERE THEIR CONSOLE WENT, WITH 70 ITEMS WAITING ON THEM: 4 pending acceptances, 21 authority-queue, 16 findings awaiting disposition, 29 sitting reviews due. THE MECHANICAL CAUSE MATTERS MORE THAN THE LAPSE: `keel serve` holds `target/release/keel.exe`, so every rebuild fails with `Access is denied` until the console is killed. I killed it for each build across this session - eight or more times - and left it down every time, because nothing in the loop asked whether the human could still see their queue. I was treating their oversight lens (D0093) as my debugging tool. TWO FIXES. (1) SERVE A COPY - `keel-serve.exe` - so builds and the console stop competing; verified by rebuilding while the console kept answering, and recorded in CLAUDE.md section 6 with the other host-friction traps. (2) A CONTROL, since 'remember to restart serve' is the manual vigilance D0047 refuses: when the model is GREEN, `keel hook stop` checks whether anything is waiting on the human and whether a console answers, and advises if not. ADVISORY, NEVER BLOCKING. Verified BOTH WAYS live: console down reported 70 items and named the command; console up was silent. validate 377 clean; check-engine clean; 36 guards ALL PASS; 191 tests; clippy clean."; }
        part storyOversightAdvisoryDoDR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800582-0814-6582-9b3e-000000300582"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
    }

    verification oaRefineGate : Test { :>> id = "0d800583-0814-6583-9b3e-000000300583"; :>> title = "Sprint 349 refine gate (DoR)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "DoR. The request had a TRIVIAL part - start the console - and a CHANGE part that is the real content: 'if there are outstanding items for me to act on, keel serve SHOULD BE RUNNING' is a standing rule about how the system behaves toward them, and a standing rule I merely remember is not a rule. Scoped as: start it now, then make the loop notice. estimatedPoints=3."; }
    part oaRefineGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800584-0814-6584-9b3e-000000300584"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }

    verification oaStandupGate : Test { :>> id = "0d800585-0814-6585-9b3e-000000300585"; :>> title = "Sprint 349 standup gate"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Approach: put the check at the TURN BOUNDARY rather than in a memory or a habit, because the turn boundary is the one place that runs whether or not I remember, and it already exists (`keel hook stop`). Two constraints on the design were non-negotiable. It must NOT BLOCK: whether the console should be up is the human's call, and a gate that blocked on it would be the over-strict control that trains its actor to disable it - and this one shares a hook with the honest-state checks, so disabling it would take those down too, which is D0132 in miniature. And it must not RE-DERIVE the obligation set: a second copy of 'which viewpoints are act-surface and what do they count' would drift from the console's, and the advisory would be about a different set than the human sees. No blockers."; }
    part oaStandupGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800586-0814-6586-9b3e-000000300586"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }

    verification oaImplementGate : Test { :>> id = "0d800587-0814-6587-9b3e-000000300587"; :>> title = "Sprint 349 implement gate"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::test; :>> procedureText = "THE CHECK ASKS `/api/version` AND REQUIRES `apiVersion` IN THE RESPONSE, rather than merely opening a socket, and that is the detail I would defend. 'Something is listening on 7777' is not the claim being made; reporting an unrelated process as a running console would be the same overstatement as issue140 and issue149, and here it would tell the human their queue is reachable when it is not. Raw TCP with std - no HTTP client dependency - with 300ms connect and 400ms read timeouts so a turn boundary can never hang on it, and any failure at all answers false, because the advisory is non-blocking so a false negative costs one redundant line while a false positive costs the human their queue. ITS COUNT COMES FROM ONE HOME: `serve::obligations_json` was extracted out of the `api_obligations` handler so the hook reads the same computation the console renders, and `obligations_total` returns `Option<i64>` where `None` means COULD NOT COMPUTE and is deliberately distinct from `Some(0)` - collapsing those is how a quiet failure becomes a false all-clear. SEPARATELY, THE BINARY-LOCK FIX: serving `keel-serve.exe`, a copy, removes the competition between the console and every rebuild - which is the actual reason the console kept dying, and no advisory would have helped if the underlying conflict remained. Verified both ways live, and controlled by two tests: one asserting the green branch of hook_stop carries no block decision and does emit a systemMessage, one asserting the reused total is computable and non-zero here."; }
    part oaImplementGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d800588-0814-6588-9b3e-000000300588"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }

    verification oaReviewGate : Test { :>> id = "0d800589-0814-6589-9b3e-000000300589"; :>> title = "Sprint 349 review gate"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Reviewed. WHAT THIS SHOWS ABOUT THE SESSION, and it is not flattering: I have spent several sprints building controls that check whether the MODEL is honest, and none that check whether the human can SEE it. The gate suite grew to 36 guards while the one surface they actually work through sat dead for hours at a time, and the thing that noticed was them asking. An oversight lens nobody verifies is a lens in name only, and D0093 says plainly that HTML is the human's oversight lens - so its availability is part of the contract, not an operational convenience. THE 70 IS ALSO A FINDING IN ITS OWN RIGHT and should not be read as merely a backlog: 21 authority-queue and 29 sitting reviews due means the human-gate side of this project has been accumulating while the AI side sprinted. I have been generating obligations faster than they can discharge them, and every sprint that ends with 'pendingAcceptances' growing makes that worse. That is not fixed by an advisory; it is a rate problem, and naming it here is the most I can do about it from this side. WHAT I DID NOT DO: I did not make the advisory start the console itself. A hook that launches a server is a hook that launches a server the human did not ask for, on a port they may be using, and the failure mode of that is worse than a message."; }
    part oaReviewGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80058a-0814-658a-9b3e-00000030058a"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }

    verification oaCloseOutGate : Test { :>> id = "0d80058b-0814-658b-9b3e-00000030058b"; :>> title = "Sprint 349 closeOut gate (autonomous, D0049)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Autonomous closeOut (D0049). issue150 recorded, triaged to dcOversightAdvisory, delivered with a control and a test in both directions. The console is UP at 127.0.0.1:7777, served from a copy so it survives further rebuilds. Doc-sync rode along: CLAUDE.md section 5 now states that a green `hook stop` also advises about the console, and section 6 records that serve holds the binary and should be served from a copy. NOT FIXED, and stated because it is the larger problem: 70 items are waiting on the human, of which 50 are authority-queue and sitting reviews. That is a rate mismatch between what I generate and what one human can discharge, and no control here addresses it. pendingAcceptances d0159, d0161, d0162, d0163. validate 377 clean; check-engine clean; 36 guards ALL PASS; 191 tests; clippy clean."; }
    part oaCloseOutGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80058c-0814-658c-9b3e-00000030058c"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }

    verification oaRetroGate : Test { :>> id = "0d80058d-0814-658d-9b3e-00000030058d"; :>> title = "Sprint 349 retro gate (autonomous, D0049)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::analyze; :>> procedureText = "Autonomous retro (D0049) under D0131's retroTrack. AVOIDABLE-ISSUE: I killed the human's console eight or more times to unblock my own builds and never once restarted it unprompted, while the queue waiting on them grew to 70. Each individual kill was correct - the build genuinely could not proceed - and the pattern was a slow failure that no single step looked like. THE TELL I MISSED IS THAT I HIT THE SAME ERROR REPEATEDLY: `Access is denied` on `keel.exe` came up again and again and I treated it each time as an obstacle to step over rather than as a design conflict to remove. A recurring error is a finding; the third occurrence should have prompted 'why do these two things compete' rather than another taskkill. The copy fix took one command and would have worked from the first occurrence. SECOND FINDING: the human's phrasing was a RULE ('should be running'), not a request, and the right response to a rule is a control rather than compliance - which is what separated this sprint from simply typing the serve command and moving on. WELL: (1) the check verifies it is a KEEL console rather than any listener, refusing the overstatement that has been this session's recurring defect class; (2) the count reuses the console's own computation rather than a second derivation; (3) advisory not blocking, argued from D0132's precedent rather than from taste; (4) the underlying binary-lock conflict was fixed too, not just monitored - an advisory over an unresolved conflict would have produced a message every turn and changed nothing; (5) the 70-item rate mismatch is named in the review gate rather than left implicit in a number. Estimate 3 pts, delivered."; }
    part oaRetroGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "0d80058e-0814-658e-9b3e-00000030058e"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::pass; :>> judgedAgainst = "PENDING"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-08-19"; :>> judgedBy = "claudeOpus5"; }
}
